{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Client -Side Coding In Asp.Net "}, {"type": "position", "content": 9}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 1}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.2"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you use when you talk about Client-Side Coding in ASP.NET. "}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Understand information about web applications, their principles and basic concepts in a range of work contexts.   Describe applications for an operating system for personal computers in a range of work contexts. "}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on web applications, and applications for operating systems for personal computers. "}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob09re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect Simple  vs. Present Perfect Continuous in Affirmative Form"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>You use the <strong>present perfect</strong> and the <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> tenses to talk about actions that started in the past but are still in progress or have just finished in the present. However, there is a remarkable difference between the meanings of both tenses. &nbsp;</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": " <p>When you use the <strong>present perfect simple</strong>, you want to indicate that an action is completed or aim to highlight the result of an action.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <ul> <li><i>She <strong>has</strong> already <strong>finished </strong>her presentation about client-side coding. (completed action)</i></li> <li><i>That programmer <strong>has used</strong> client-side scripts to code the controls on that page. (what)</i></li> <li><i>My students <strong>have sent</strong> me thousands of questions about client-side programming. (how much)</i></li> <li><i>I <strong>have read</strong> that article about client-side programming twice. (how often)</i></li> </ul>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>On the other hand, you use the <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> to highlight the duration of an action that is in progress.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Examples:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <ul> <li><i>The programmer <strong>has been working</strong> on that code for hours. (how long)</i></li> <li><i>I <strong>have been preparing</strong> a presentation about client-side coding. (That&rsquo;s what I&rsquo;ve been doing, it doesn&rsquo;t matter if the presentation is finished or not)</i></li> </ul> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>The following are the structures you need to use when building affirmative sentences with both structures:</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Present Perfect Simple</strong></p> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject + <strong>have/ has</strong> + <strong>main verb in past participle</strong> + complement.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p><strong>Present Perfect Continuous</strong></p> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Subject +<strong> have/ has</strong> +<strong> been</strong> +<strong> main verb in ING</strong> + complement.</p> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p> <p>It&rsquo;s important to note that there are some verbs that cannot be used with the <strong>present perfect continuous</strong> but only with the <strong>present simple tense.</strong> Take a look at the chart below:</p>"}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":3,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<center>Thinking\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<center>Senses\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<center>State\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"\u2022  Think<br> \u2022\\tBelieve <br>\u2022\\tKnow <br>\u2022\\tUnderstand<br>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"\u2022\\tFeel<br> \u2022\\tSmell<br> \u2022\\tTaste <br>\u2022\\tSee<br> \u2022\\tHear <br>\u2022\\tTouch<br>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"\u2022\\tBe<br> \u2022\\tHave (possession)\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "So far, we have been exploring different ASP.NET programming features, but we haven't given much attention to client-side programming on this platform."}, {"type": "example", "content": "ASP.NET developers have been working hard to make server controls be able to execute scripts through different events."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Well, as we have discussed in recent sessions, there are specifically two types of files in which ASP.NET pages are written: the content file or markup file (.aspx) and the code-behind file."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Simple Present Passive in Questions"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "<p>We use sentences in <strong>simple present passive</strong> or <strong>present passive voice</strong> when we want to stand out the function of an <strong>object</strong> in a sentence rather than the<strong> subject</strong>. This tense is commonly used when we expect to provide explanations.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>When we aim to highlight the person or element that performs an action, we use&nbsp;<i>active voice</i>.</p> <p><br /> Examples:</p> <ul> <li><i><strong>ASP.NET pages and applications</strong> use client-based tools. </i></li> <li><i> Do <strong>ASP.NET pages and applications</strong> use client-based tools?</i></li> </ul> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>But when it comes to highlighting the role of the object in a sentence, we need to use the&nbsp;<i>passive voice</i>.</p> <p>&nbsp;</p> <p>Examples: <br /> <br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &bull; <strong><i>Client-based tools</i></strong><i>&nbsp;are used by ASP.NET pages and applications.</i><br />&nbsp; &nbsp; &bull; <i> Are <strong>client-based tools&nbsp;</i></strong><i>used by ASP.NET pages and applications?</i></p> <p><i><br /> </i>When using the passive voice the subject could be omitted, but in case you wish to mention it, you need to use the particle&nbsp;<strong>by</strong>&nbsp;followed by the noun.</p>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<p>This is the grammatical structure we need to use when building <strong>interrogative </strong>sentences in&nbsp;<strong>simple present passive</strong>:&nbsp;</p> <p style=\"text-align: center;\">Wh-word (optional) + <strong>Verb be</strong> + Object + <strong>Verb in past participle</strong> + Complement?</p> <p style=\"text-align: left;\"><br /> <br />Examples:</p> <ul> <li><strong><i>Are</i></strong><i> the client-side scripts <strong>executed</strong> in the browser or in the server?</i></li> <li><strong><i>Is</i></strong><i> client-side coding <strong>provided</strong> by ASP.NET pages?</i></li> <li><i>Where <strong>are</strong> the client-side scripts <strong>executed</strong>?</i></li> <li><i>How <strong>are</strong> events <strong>raised</strong> by server controls?</i></li> </ul> <p><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "What aspects are included in client-side coding?"}, {"type": "example", "content": "How is client source code written? "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "post back "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the process of sending an ASP.NET page to the server for processing "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: So, ASP.NET provides different server controls that use client-side scripting in order to respond to the users' request without needing to send a post back to the server. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob09aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "script "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a series of instructions or commands that need to be interpreted by a specific program "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: ASP.NET developers have been working hard to make server controls be able to execute scripts through different events. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob09aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "mark-up file "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a file that possesses all the logical structure of pages"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: There are specifically two types of files in which ASP.NET pages are written: the content file or markup file (.aspx) and the code-behind file."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob09aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "class definition  "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "the setting of methods and variables that will be used in an object "}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: The content file holds the structure of a page, which is the whole of control tags of the page; and the code-behind file has the class definition."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n6le01ob09aud04"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "assembly "}, {"type": "definition", "content": "a runtime or deployment unit"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: As a result of the combined data of this class, the class definition, and the system generated code, we get the executable code or assembly to process clients' requests."}, 